==Legacy==

===Military influence===

Though short-lived, the Cerdanyan Legion had a lasting influence on Baliscan military organization and doctrine. The concept of specialized peacekeeping units, combining military and administrative functions, was revived in later conflicts and eventually institutionalized in the [[Confederal Peacekeeping Service]] established in 1912.

The uniform of the Zuau influenced the dress of later Baliscan light infantry units, particularly the [[Mountain Rifles]] ({{lang|bl|Caçadors de Muntanya}}) formed in 1845. Elements of the distinctive dress, particularly the cap and sash, were retained as ceremonial uniform for certain units.

The "Legion Code" of conduct for peacekeeping operations was studied and adapted by later military formations. The emphasis on restraint, de-escalation, and cultural sensitivity became standard doctrine for Baliscan forces engaged in internal security and peacekeeping missions.

===Cultural impact===

The Zuau became an important symbol for the Imfezzan community in Balisca. The unit demonstrated the contributions of Imfezzan to the Baliscan state and helped integrate the community into the national identity. Veterans of the Legion formed the core of Imfezzan political and social organizations in the decades after disbandment.

In popular culture, the Zuau was romanticized in literature and art. The distinctive uniform and the romantic image of the Imfezzan soldier became subjects of paintings, songs, and stories. The Legion was portrayed as a force of noble warriors maintaining peace in a troubled land, an image that sometimes obscured the more complex realities of its service.

The term "zuau" entered the Baliscan vocabulary as a synonym for a peacekeeper or mediator, particularly one seen as neutral and fair. The phrase "sending in the zuau" became idiomatic for intervention in a dispute by an impartial third party.

===Historical assessment===

Historians have offered varying assessments of the Cerdanyan Legion. Some have praised the unit as an innovative and effective solution to a complex conflict, arguing that its disbandment was premature and contributed to later violence. Others have criticized the Legion as an instrument of confederal overreach and a violation of provincial autonomy.

The ethnic dimension of the Legion has also been debated. Some scholars have argued that the heavy reliance on Imfezzan troops reflected a cynical exploitation of a marginalized community for dangerous duty. Others have countered that service in the Legion provided economic opportunity and social advancement for Imfezzan immigrants, and that the unit was a source of pride for the community.

Regardless of these debates, the Zuau remains a significant chapter in Baliscan military and social history, representing both the challenges of the early confederal period and the contributions of immigrant communities to the Baliscan nation.

